  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186068695,
      "id": "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c",
      "title": "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/7882e336e826114cd8fd0b4bb33674ed9eac75a9bb360137abccee3a9031dcde.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-5$\n- **II.** $x=-3$\n- **III.** $x=-1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-5.$ Since there is a jump at $x=-5,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-5$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ Since there is a break at $x=-3,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/a43cff4e1f3aaf8dca9a09bf9d73e8b902421c0c280d45b2a3fdd833edc0e3bd.png)",
