    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/0741dff1bec1d4ee9cbd922bd0d3606630d98d3f49fede75bb76eb569780d837.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-4$\n\n- **II.** $x=0$\n\n- **III.** $x=4$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-4.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-4,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
