      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=6$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=8.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=8,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=8$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/2284bb6b7674847b0569780a979b643bee70a77a824990b2eef06ba6301551d6.png)",
