      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/66a9af0ff9c597847da9d8256aead0924d158e84b4b77b7d445047b896e534a2.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-5$\n- **II.** $x=1$\n- **III.** $x=5$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-5.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-5$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=5.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=5,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=5$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/99a443897b3c8d36fe20ac528f9b875098e0bef8c09a22707cf6cf6961aca438.png)",
