  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186068695,
      "id": "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c",
      "title": "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/88ca858b927202d23e42162e97c225c59cc7dbcd3b7881ef5678a20f860d3903.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-3$\n- **II.** $x=0$\n- **III.** $x=2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=2,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/7743f62900d78519cbc8271121923efd97715f9960ac25ac1aaae9fcb625be38.png)",
