    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/7a4698a192454d1b915d00a7cd45425a9ce3d31fd834b663a8835acb8588d68d.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-3$\n\n- **II.** $x=-1$\n\n- **III.** $x=1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-3,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
